[s0l0!-header][h1]Computed Property Names[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

With ES6, you can now use [b]computed property[/b] names. Using the square bracket notation [], we can use an expression for a property name, including concatenating strings. This can be useful in cases where we want to create certain objects based on user data (e.g. id, email, and so on).

Here are three examples:
[s0l0!-header][b]Example 1[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1210"]let prop = 'name';
let id = '1234';
let mobile = '08923';

let user = {
  [prop]: 'Jack',
  [`user_${id}`]: `${mobile}`
};[/code]
[s0l0!-header][b]Example 2:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1207"]var i = 0;
var a = {
  ['foo' + ++i]: i,
  ['foo' + ++i]: i,
  ['foo' + ++i]: i
};[/code]
[s0l0!-header][b]Example 3:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1608"]var param = 'size';
var config = {
  [param]: 12,
  ['mobile' + param.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + param.slice(1)]: 4
};
console.log(config.mobileSize);[/code]
[note]It is very useful when you need to create custom objects based on some variables.[/note]

